<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wabash River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wabash River flows about 810 kilometres from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> across Indiana to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>, forming part of the border between Indiana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. Its valley was home to the Miami and other Native peoples and was settled by the French at Vincennes. In 1791 the Western Confederacy routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-st-clair">Arthur St. Clair</a>'s army near its headwaters, and in 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa">Tenskwatawa</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a> near its junction with the Tippecanoe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wabash-river.jpg" alt="Wabash River" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wabashrivermap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
